    Assumption challenge:
      Tournament vertices need not be row entries or runners.  Considered vertices
      were odd moments, u-carriers, Bernoulli denominators, exact towers, cuboid
      packings, simplex-packing analogues, LRC proof obligations, and support lifts.
      The chosen quotient preserves the balance equation, odd-moment ledger,
      exact p=1/p=2 tower recovery, and transfer channels.  It destroys individual
      endpoint residues and any actual LRC/unit-distance/code witness.
      Challenged assumption: the scalar anchor is not the proof object; the proof
      object is the retained odd-moment/support address that explains when the
      anchor stays integral and when it drifts into Bernoulli fractions.

[6] Next proof tasks
    1. Prove the expansion with a uniform remainder in u for fixed p.
    2. Prove the HYP-2454 integer bracket for p>=3 using the odd-moment formula.
    3. Lift the odd-moment ledger to LRC14: odd walls, owner/carry fibers, and support.
    4. Compare simplex-in-cuboid packing constants with higher Faulhaber carriers.
    5. Search for a finite-dimensional residue/tournament quotient that detects
       Bernoulli denominator drift before the full polynomial is expanded.
